    @section mlalgo_anomaly Anomaly Detection

    Catch fraudulent transactions, equipment failures, and security breaches by automatically
    identifying data points that don't fit normal patterns.

    - @ref Qore::ML::IsolationForest "IsolationForest" — Detects globally unusual records by measuring
      how easy they are to separate from the rest. A \$50,000 transaction when most are under \$500
      is isolated quickly and scores near 1.0 (highly anomalous). Best for broad screening across
      all data. Uses random partitioning; anomalous points are isolated in fewer splits.
    - @ref Qore::ML::LOF "LOF" — Detects records that are unusual relative to their local neighborhood.
      A \$200 purchase may be normal for premium customers but anomalous for budget customers; LOF
      catches this by comparing local density. Best when "unusual" depends on context. Uses
      k-nearest neighbors to measure local density deviation.

    @section mlalgo_clustering Clustering

    Discover customer segments, group related events, and classify data points — either into
    hard groups or with probability scores reflecting genuine uncertainty.

    - @ref Qore::ML::DBSCAN "DBSCAN" — Discovers natural groups without requiring you to specify how
      many exist. Groups can have any shape: geographic delivery zones, clusters of related
      support tickets, or network traffic hotspots. Points that don't belong to any group are
      labeled as noise.
    - @ref Qore::ML::KMeans "KMeans" — Segments data into a specified number of groups — e.g., divide
      10,000 customers into 5 spending tiers or classify products into 3 price categories. Uses
      k-means++ initialization and supports online (mini-batch) updates so segments evolve as
      new data arrives.
    - @ref Qore::ML::GMM "GMM" — Assigns each record a probability for each group rather than a hard
      label. A customer might be 70%% "budget shopper" and 30%% "impulse buyer," reflecting
      genuine overlap. Uses Gaussian mixtures (EM algorithm) and supports full, diagonal, and
      spherical covariance types. Supports online learning via incremental EM updates.

    @section mlalgo_classification Classification

    Assign records to categories — approve or deny, spam or legitimate, which product
    category, which risk tier. Classification is the most common supervised ML task.

    - @ref Qore::ML::LogisticRegression "LogisticRegression" — Learns a decision boundary to
      separate categories. For example, approve or deny loan applications based on income, credit
      score, and debt ratio. Handles two categories (binary) or many categories (multiclass via
      one-vs-rest). Reports the probability of each category, not just the prediction — so you
      can flag borderline cases (e.g., 52%% approve) for manual review. Supports L2
      regularization to prevent overfitting. Supports online learning via mini-batch
      SGD updates for continuous model refinement on streaming data.
    - @ref Qore::ML::KNN "KNN" — Classifies each record based on its most similar neighbors in
      the training data. For example, if 4 of the 5 most similar past customers churned, predict
      this customer will too. Also works for regression (predict a number as the average of
      neighbors' values). Simple, interpretable, and no training phase — but slower on large
      datasets since it compares against every training record.

    @section mlalgo_trees Tree-Based Algorithms

    The enterprise ML workhorses for tabular data — decision trees, random forests, and
    gradient boosting dominate fraud detection, churn prediction, credit scoring, and
    virtually every structured data competition.

    - @ref Qore::ML::DecisionTree "DecisionTree" — Learns a tree of if/then rules that
      split data into groups. For example, "if income > 50K and credit_score > 700, approve
      the loan." Supports Gini impurity, entropy, and MSE criteria. Reports feature
      importances showing which fields matter most. Fast to train and fully interpretable.
    - @ref Qore::ML::RandomForest "RandomForest" — Trains many decision trees on random
      subsets of the data and features, then combines their votes. More accurate than a
      single tree because individual trees' mistakes cancel out. Configurable via n_trees,
      max_features ("sqrt" or "log2"), and bootstrap sampling.
    - @ref Qore::ML::GradientBoostedTrees "GradientBoostedTrees" — Builds trees
      sequentially, each one correcting the previous ensemble's errors. The standard choice
      for tabular data in production (equivalent to XGBoost/LightGBM). Supports binary
      classification (log-loss), regression (MSE), early stopping with validation set, and
      row subsampling for stochastic gradient boosting.

    @section mlalgo_svm_nb SVM and Naive Bayes

    Classic statistical classifiers — SVM for maximum-margin linear and non-linear
    classification, Naive Bayes for fast probabilistic classification.

    - @ref Qore::ML::SVM "SVM" — Finds the decision boundary that maximizes the margin
      between classes. For example, separate legitimate from fraudulent transactions by
      finding the widest gap in feature space. Supports linear and RBF (Gaussian) kernels.
      Multiclass via one-vs-one voting with confidence probabilities. Regularization via C
      parameter prevents overfitting.
    - @ref Qore::ML::NaiveBayes "NaiveBayes" — Assumes features are independent given the
      class label, then uses Bayes' theorem to compute class probabilities. Despite the
      "naive" independence assumption, often competitive for text classification, spam
      filtering, and medical diagnosis. Extremely fast to train and supports online learning
      (incremental updates) via Welford's algorithm for streaming variance computation.

    @section mlalgo_regression Regression

    Predict prices, estimate delivery times, and forecast demand by learning relationships
    between input features and a target value.

    - @ref Qore::ML::LinearRegression "LinearRegression" — Learns the best straight-line relationship
      between inputs and a target. For example, predict a property's price from square footage,
      number of bedrooms, and neighborhood — the model reports which features matter most
      (coefficients), the baseline (intercept), and prediction accuracy (R-squared). Supports
      online learning via SGD updates for continuous model refinement.

    @section mlalgo_timeseries Time Series

    Forecast future values and understand what drives change in data with seasonal patterns,
    such as monthly revenue, daily server load, or weekly inventory levels.

    - @ref Qore::ML::HoltWinters "HoltWinters" — Forecasts values by learning trend (going up or down)
      and seasonal patterns (e.g., revenue peaks every December, server load spikes Monday
      mornings). Supports additive and multiplicative seasonality with optional damped trends.
    - @ref Qore::ML::SeasonalDecomposition "SeasonalDecomposition" — Separates a time series into three
      parts: the underlying growth trend, the repeating seasonal pattern (e.g., holiday shopping
      cycles), and random fluctuations. Helps answer "is this increase due to real growth or just
      a seasonal spike?" Supports additive and multiplicative models.

    @section mlalgo_dimreduce Dimensionality Reduction

    Simplify complex data for visualization, noise removal, and faster downstream analysis.

    - @ref Qore::ML::PCA "PCA" — Compresses many fields into fewer combined values that capture the
      most important patterns. For example, reduce 50 sensor readings to 3 key values that
      explain 95%% of the variance, making it practical to visualize equipment health on a 3D
      plot. Supports fixed component count or automatic selection via variance threshold.

    @section mlalgo_preprocess Data Preprocessing

    Clean and prepare data before analysis. Most ML algorithms give poor results when fields
    have very different ranges or when records have missing values — preprocessing fixes both.

    - @ref Qore::ML::StandardScaler "StandardScaler" — Makes fields with different ranges
      directly comparable by adjusting each one so it centers around zero with similar spread.
      For example, if "age" ranges from 18–65 and "income" ranges from 20,000–200,000,
      standardizing them prevents income from dominating the analysis. Use before clustering
      (KMeans, DBSCAN), anomaly detection (IsolationForest, LOF), or dimensionality reduction
      (PCA).
    - @ref Qore::ML::MinMaxScaler "MinMaxScaler" — Converts field values to fit within a
      specific range (default 0.0 to 1.0). For example, temperatures from -20°C to 45°C become
      0.0 to 1.0, making them easy to compare with humidity or wind speed. Useful for heat maps,
      scoring dashboards, or feeding data into pre-trained ONNX models that expect normalized
      input. Supports inverse transformation to recover original values.
    - @ref Qore::ML::Imputer "Imputer" — Automatically fills in missing values so downstream
      analysis can process complete records. Strategies: \c "mean" (average — best for
      temperatures, response times), \c "median" (middle value — better when outliers are present,
      e.g., transaction amounts), \c "most_frequent" (most common value — best for status codes
      or ratings), \c "constant" (a fixed number you choose, e.g., 0 or -1 as a sentinel).
    - @ref Qore::ML::MLPipeline "MLPipeline" — Chains multiple preprocessing steps and an
      analysis algorithm into one unit that you fit and predict with in a single call. For
      example, chain a StandardScaler with KMeans so that new data is always scaled the same
      way before being assigned to a cluster. Prevents inconsistencies between training and
      production.

    @section mlalgo_feature_eng Feature Engineering

    Transform raw data into forms suitable for ML algorithms. Encode categorical variables,
    select informative features, and ensure consistency between training and production.

    - @ref Qore::ML::OneHotEncoder "OneHotEncoder" — Converts integer-encoded categorical
      features into binary columns. Each unique value becomes its own column with 0/1 values.
      For example, a "color" column with values 0 (red), 1 (green), 2 (blue) becomes three
      columns: \c color_0, \c color_1, \c color_2. Use \c drop_first to avoid multicollinearity
      in linear models by dropping the first category (the reference level).
    - @ref Qore::ML::LabelEncoder "LabelEncoder" — Maps string or integer category values
      to consecutive integers 0, 1, 2, ..., n_classes-1. For example, ("red", "green", "blue")
      becomes (2, 1, 0) in alphabetical order. Supports inverse transformation back to original
      values. Unknown/null values are encoded as -1.
    - @ref Qore::ML::VarianceThreshold "VarianceThreshold" — Removes features with low
      variance (population variance). Features with variance at or below the threshold are
      removed during transform(). Default threshold 0.0 removes only constant (zero-variance)
      features. Use to eliminate uninformative columns before feeding data into algorithms.
    - @ref Qore::ML::PolynomialFeatures "PolynomialFeatures" — Generates polynomial and
      interaction terms from input features. For example, with degree=2 and features [a, b],
      produces [a, b, a^2, a*b, b^2]. Enables linear models to capture non-linear
      relationships (polynomial regression). Supports \c interaction_only mode (no powers)
      and optional bias column.
    - @ref Qore::ML::DateTimeFeatures "DateTimeFeatures" — Extracts numeric components from
      date/datetime values: year, month, day, hour, minute, second, day_of_week, day_of_year,
      quarter, is_weekend, week_of_year. Use to let ML models learn temporal patterns
      (e.g., delivery times vary by day of week and hour).
    - @ref Qore::ML::TextFeatures "TextFeatures" — Converts text documents into numeric
      feature vectors using TF-IDF weighting or raw word counts (bag-of-words). Use to
      turn customer reviews, support tickets, or log messages into numbers that ML
      algorithms can process. Configurable vocabulary size and minimum document frequency.
    - @ref Qore::ML::SelectKBest "SelectKBest" — Selects the top-k most informative features
      based on a scoring function. Supports ANOVA F-statistic (\c "f_classif") for
      classification tasks and discretized mutual information (\c "mutual_info") for any
      target type. Reports per-feature scores and a boolean support mask.
    - @ref Qore::ML::RFE "RFE" — Recursive Feature Elimination: iteratively fits a decision
      tree, ranks features by importance (Gini/MSE reduction), removes the least important
      feature(s), and repeats until the desired number remain. A wrapper-based method that
      often produces better results than filter methods. Reports per-feature ranking
      (1 = selected, 2+ = elimination round).

    @section mlalgo_stats Statistical Functions

    Descriptive statistics, hypothesis testing, and probability distribution functions
    for data exploration and validation.

    <b>Descriptive statistics</b>:
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_percentile() "ml_percentile()" — compute any percentile (e.g., median = 50th percentile)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_iqr() "ml_iqr()" — interquartile range (75th - 25th percentile) for outlier detection
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_skewness() "ml_skewness()" — measure distribution asymmetry (0 = symmetric)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_kurtosis() "ml_kurtosis()" — measure tail heaviness (excess kurtosis; 0 = normal)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_correlation_matrix() "ml_correlation_matrix()" — Pearson correlation between all feature pairs
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_covariance_matrix() "ml_covariance_matrix()" — covariance between all feature pairs

    <b>Hypothesis testing</b>:
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_t_test() "ml_t_test()" — Welch's two-sample t-test with exact p-values via Cephes incbet (regularized incomplete beta function, same as scipy)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_chi_squared_test() "ml_chi_squared_test()" — chi-squared test of independence for contingency tables

    <b>Normal distribution</b>:
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_normal_pdf() "ml_normal_pdf()" — probability density function
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_normal_cdf() "ml_normal_cdf()" — cumulative distribution function
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_normal_ppf() "ml_normal_ppf()" — percent point function (inverse CDF, quantile function)

    @section mlalgo_drift Concept Drift Detection

    Monitor deployed models for performance degradation. When the data distribution
    changes (concept drift), a model trained on historical data may give incorrect
    predictions. These detectors tell you when to retrain.

    - @ref Qore::ML::ADWIN "ADWIN" — Maintains an adaptive window of observations and
      detects distribution changes by comparing sub-window means. Automatically drops
      outdated data when drift is detected. O(log N) memory via exponential histogram
      compression. Best for general-purpose monitoring of any numeric metric (prediction
      error, latency, accuracy, revenue).
    - @ref Qore::ML::PageHinkley "PageHinkley" — Detects mean shifts by tracking the
      cumulative deviation from the running mean. When the deviation exceeds a threshold
      (lambda), drift is signaled. Simple and efficient — good for monitoring a single
      metric like average prediction error or transaction amount.
    - @ref Qore::ML::DDM "DDM" — Monitors a classifier's error rate using binomial
      statistics. Provides two levels: \c warning (possible drift, collect more data)
      and \c drift (confirmed, retrain now). Feed 0 for correct predictions, 1 for
      errors. Best for directly monitoring classification model accuracy.

    @section mlalgo_evaluation Model Evaluation

    Measure how well your models perform — without objective metrics you can't know whether
    a model is good enough for production or which of two models is better.

    <b>Classification metrics</b> (for models that predict categories):
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_accuracy() "ml_accuracy()" — fraction of correct predictions (e.g., 0.95 = 95%% right)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_classification_report() "ml_classification_report()" — detailed breakdown with precision, recall, and F1 per class
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_confusion_matrix() "ml_confusion_matrix()" — shows where the model confuses one class for another

    <b>Regression metrics</b> (for models that predict numbers):
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_mse() "ml_mse()" / @ref Qore::ML::ml_rmse() "ml_rmse()" — average squared/root error (lower is better)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_mae() "ml_mae()" — average absolute error, in the same units as your data
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_r2_score() "ml_r2_score()" — how much of the variation your model explains (1.0 = perfect)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_explained_variance() "ml_explained_variance()" — similar to R² but accounts for bias

    <b>Clustering metrics</b> (for models that group data):
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_silhouette_score() "ml_silhouette_score()" — how well-separated the groups are (-1 to 1; above 0.5 is good)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_davies_bouldin_score() "ml_davies_bouldin_score()" — how compact and separated the groups are (lower is better)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_calinski_harabasz_score() "ml_calinski_harabasz_score()" — ratio of between-group to within-group variance (higher is better)

    <b>Cross-validation</b>:
    - @ref Qore::ML::CrossValidator "CrossValidator" — splits your data into k folds for
      train/test evaluation, so you can check whether a model generalizes to unseen data

    @section mlalgo_onnx ONNX Model Inference

    Run complex models (neural networks, gradient-boosted trees, etc.) trained in Python, R, or
    other frameworks without needing those tools at runtime.

    When compiled with ONNX Runtime support, the module provides:
    - @ref Qore::ML::OnnxModel "OnnxModel" — Loads and runs pre-trained ONNX models for inference.
      For example, a credit scoring model trained in scikit-learn can be exported to ONNX and
      deployed in a %Qore pipeline for real-time scoring. Supports float, double, int8, int16,
      int32, int64, and bool tensor types. ONNX Runtime sessions are thread-safe for concurrent
      inference. The \c run(), \c runTensors(), and \c runBatch() methods accept optional
      output tensor names so multi-output models can materialize only the values needed by
      the caller.
    - @ref Qore::ML::OnnxSessionPool "OnnxSessionPool" — Keeps a bounded set of immutable
      ONNX sessions ready for concurrent service requests. Use it when production traffic needs
      explicit limits for session count, concurrent runs, queue depth, and acquisition timeout.
      The pool also supports ordered \c runBatch() chunking with \c dynamic_batch_size so large
      request lists can be split into stable mini-batches without changing result order. The
      Future-returning \c runAsync(), \c runTensorsAsync(), and \c runBatchAsync() methods let
      service handlers submit work without blocking; when \c dynamic_batch_size and
      \c dynamic_batch_wait_ms are configured, concurrent \c runAsync() callers are coalesced
      into ordered batches and each Future resolves to its own result.
    - @ref Qore::ML::Tensor "Tensor" — Dense tensor wrapper around \c buffer<T> plus explicit
      shape metadata. Use it with @ref Qore::ML::OnnxModel::runTensors() "OnnxModel::runTensors()"
      to avoid converting large numeric inputs and outputs through nested lists.

    To check ONNX availability at runtime:
    @code{.py}
hash<Qore::ML::MLCapabilities> caps = Qore::ML::ml_get_capabilities();
if (caps.has_onnx) {
    Qore::ML::OnnxModel model("/path/to/model.onnx");
    hash<auto> result = model.run({"input": data});
    hash<auto> scores = model.run({"input": data}, ("probabilities",));

    Qore::ML::Tensor dense(data: new buffer<float32>((1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0)), shape: (2, 2));
    hash<auto> tensor_result = model.runTensors({"input": dense}, ("output",));
    Qore::ML::Tensor output = tensor_result.output;
    printf("output dtype=%s shape=%y\n", output.getDtype(), output.getShape());

    Qore::ML::OnnxSessionPool pool("/path/to/model.onnx",
        <Qore::ML::OnnxSessionConfig>{"providers": ()}, {
            "max_sessions": 4,
            "queue_depth": 64,
            "timeout_ms": 250,
            "dynamic_batch_size": 128,
            "dynamic_batch_wait_ms": 2,
        });
    hash<auto> pooled_result = pool.run({"input": data});
    hash<auto> pooled_scores = pool.run({"input": data}, ("probabilities",));
    Future<hash<auto>> pooled_future = pool.runAsync({"input": data});
    hash<auto> async_result = pooled_future.get(5s);
    hash<auto> pool_stats = pool.getPoolStats();

    # Pack dense DataFrame columns directly into a row-major model tensor.
    Qore::ML::Tensor features = Qore::ML::Tensor::fromColumns((
        df.getColumnBuffer("income"),
        df.getColumnBuffer("debt_ratio"),
        df.getColumnBuffer("history_months"),
    ), "float32");

    # Reshape and row-slice tensors without materializing nested lists.
    Qore::ML::Tensor flat = features.reshape((features.size(),));
    Qore::ML::Tensor first_batch = features.sliceRows(offset: 0, count: 128);
    Qore::ML::Tensor next_batch = features.sliceRows(offset: 128, count: 128);
    Qore::ML::Tensor combined = Qore::ML::Tensor::concatRows((first_batch, next_batch));

    # Postprocess ONNX transformer outputs while they are still dense tensors.
    # "last_hidden_state" has shape (batch, tokens, embedding_dim); "attention_mask"
    # has shape (batch, tokens). The result has shape (batch, embedding_dim).
    Qore::ML::Tensor input_ids(data: new buffer<int64>((101, 2023, 2003, 102)), shape: (1, 4));
    Qore::ML::Tensor attention_mask(data: new buffer<int64>((1, 1, 1, 1)), shape: (1, 4));
    hash<auto> token_outputs = model.runTensors({
        "input_ids": input_ids,
        "attention_mask": attention_mask,
    });
    Qore::ML::Tensor sentence_embeddings = token_outputs.last_hidden_state
        .poolTokenEmbeddings(attention_mask: attention_mask, strategy: "mean")
        .l2NormalizeRows();

    # Classifier and reranker logits can also be postprocessed natively.
    Qore::ML::Tensor logits(data: new buffer<float32>((0.2, 2.4, -0.7, 1.1, 0.4, 3.2)), shape: (2, 3));
    Qore::ML::Tensor probabilities = logits.sigmoid();
    Qore::ML::Tensor labels = logits.argmax(axis: -1);
    hash<auto> top3 = logits.topK(k: 2, axis: -1);

    # Split rank-2 ONNX outputs directly into dense DataFrame columns.
    list<auto> probability_columns = logits.toColumnBuffers();
    DataFrame::DataFrame scored({
        "class_0": probability_columns[0],
        "class_1": probability_columns[1],
        "class_2": probability_columns[2],
    });
}
    @endcode

    @section mlalgo_onnx_identity Loaded-Content Identity

    A caller that must prove which model produced a result needs to identify the content the
    session actually loaded. A model path, file size, modification time or execution provider name
    does not identify that content, and neither does a digest of the \c .onnx bytes alone: when a
    model stores tensors in external data files, identical model bytes produce different results
    depending on files the model only refers to.

    @ref Qore::ML::OnnxModel::getContentIdentity() reports an immutable, versioned identity that
    binds the model graph bytes and every external tensor byte the session consumed. Capture is
    requested when the model is loaded:

    @code{.py}
ML::OnnxModel model(path, <ML::OnnxSessionConfig>{"content_identity": "required"});
hash<ML::OnnxContentIdentity> identity = model.getContentIdentity();
printf("content: %s (%d external dependencies)\n", identity.digest, identity.dependencies.size());
    @endcode

    Capture modes:

    |!Value|!Behavior
    |\c "disabled"|default; nothing is captured and the reported identity is \c "unknown"
    |\c "best_effort"|content is captured when it can be bound; otherwise an \c "unknown" identity with a reason is reported
    |\c "required"|as \c "best_effort", but loading fails with \c ML-ONNX-CONTENT-IDENTITY-ERROR when a complete identity cannot be established

    Properties of the identity:
    - It is captured while the session is created and never recomputed from the model source, so
      replacing the model files afterwards cannot change what an already-loaded session reports.
    - When capture is enabled, the model graph and every external tensor file are read by Qore and
      handed to ONNX Runtime, so the digest describes exactly the bytes the session loaded; there
      is no window in which a file could be substituted between validation and use.
    - It is content-only: relocating a model package or rewriting it with identical bytes does not
      change the identity, while any change to the model graph or to a consumed external byte
      range does.
    - It contains no model content, local paths or declared dependency filenames, so it is safe in
      caller-facing diagnostics.
    - An identity that cannot be fully established is reported as \c "unknown" with a reason and
      carries no digest; a size, timestamp, path or provider name is never substituted for it.

    External tensor data declared outside the model's top-level initializers (in a subgraph, a
    local function body, a node attribute or a sparse tensor) is resolved by ONNX Runtime from the
    model's directory and cannot be attributed to the loaded session. Such a model still loads
    exactly as it would without capture, and reports an \c "unknown" identity.

    Tokenizer configuration and any preprocessing or pooling policy applied around the model are
    not part of this identity; a producer that applies them must bind them separately.

    @ref Qore::ML::OnnxSessionPool::getContentIdentity() reports the identity shared by all pooled
    sessions. Each pooled session loads the model independently, so if the content changes while
    the pool is being created, the pool has no single identity: with \c "required" the pool
    constructor fails, and otherwise an \c "unknown" identity is reported.

    @note Capturing content identity holds the model graph and all external tensor data in memory
    for the lifetime of the session, which is why it is disabled by default.

    @section mlalgo_onnx_device GPU Device Binding (Zero-Copy)

    On builds with a device-capable execution provider (CUDA first), ONNX inputs and
    outputs can stay resident on the accelerator instead of always copying through host
    memory. Device binding is opt-in and never changes CPU-only behavior: on a CPU
    provider, or when a requested device path is unavailable, the policy resolves to host
    memory (optionally with an explicit error instead of a silent fallback).

    Device binding is configured through the @ref Qore::ML::OnnxDeviceBindingConfig
    "device_binding" field of @ref Qore::ML::OnnxSessionConfig "OnnxSessionConfig":

    |!Field|!Meaning
    |\c enabled|Enables device-aware input/output binding.
    |\c default_output_device|\c "cpu", \c "provider", or an explicit \c "<provider>:<device_id>" such as \c "cuda:0".
    |\c allow_host_fallback|Allow host copies when a requested device path is unavailable (default \c False).
    |\c materialize_outputs|Force returned device outputs to host before returning (default \c False).
    |\c require_zero_copy_inputs|Reject device inputs that cannot be bound without a host copy.
    |\c require_zero_copy_outputs|Reject outputs that cannot be returned as device-backed buffers.

    @subsection mlalgo_onnx_device_provider_classes Provider Classes

    Execution providers fall into three groups for the purposes of device binding:

    - <b>Raw-device-memory providers</b> (CUDA, TensorRT, ROCm) expose device
      pointers that ONNX Runtime can bind outputs to directly. Device-binding requests
      flow through to the provider and outputs come back as device-backed
      @ref Qore::ML::Tensor "Tensors".
    - <b>EP-acceleration providers</b> (CoreML on macOS, OpenVINO, DirectML,
      WebGPU) accelerate inference on a non-CPU device but manage their own opaque
      memory; user code cannot bind to a raw device pointer. A device-binding request
      with one of these as the active provider <b>resolves to host</b>: outputs come
      back to host memory, no fabricated host/device transfers are reported, and the
      \c provider_host_resolutions counter is incremented so the reduction is
      observable. This is intentional and matches the "truthful zero-copy" design
      principle -- no error is raised even when \c allow_host_fallback is False, because
      the EP acceleration is itself the device path being honored.
    - <b>CPU provider</b> -- a device-binding request against CPU is a configuration
      error and raises \c ML-ONNX-DEVICE-UNAVAILABLE unless \c allow_host_fallback is
      True.

    @subsection mlalgo_onnx_device_macos Apple Silicon (CoreML + Metal/UMA)

    macOS / Apple Silicon has two distinct device paths, and the contract is
    different from CUDA because of the Apple **Unified Memory Architecture (UMA):**
    the CPU and GPU share one physical memory pool, so a "device buffer" can be
    the same physical bytes as its host view.

    - **CoreML EP** is the primary accelerator: it dispatches inference to the
      ANE, GPU, or CPU and manages its own opaque memory. CoreML is treated as
      an EP-acceleration provider (above) -- a device-binding request resolves
      to host with zero transfers and a \c provider_host_resolutions bump.
      Outputs are returned as host @ref Qore::ML::Tensor "Tensors".

    - **Metal/UMA buffers** (via @ref Qore::ML::Tensor::toDevice()
      "Tensor::toDevice(\"metal\")") allocate an \c MTLBuffer with
      \c MTLStorageModeShared. On UMA, the CPU and GPU see the bytes at the same
      physical address, so binding a Metal-tagged input to any provider (CPU,
      CoreML, WebGPU) is zero-copy through CPU memory info. The transfer
      counters report **zero** host↔device transfers truthfully -- no DMA hop
      ever occurs. \c materialize_outputs is effectively free on UMA.

    - @ref Qore::ML::Tensor::pinnedHost() "Tensor::pinnedHost()" is moot on
      Apple Silicon (there is no DMA hop to accelerate). The Apple build returns
      a Tensor sharing the same host buffer (no allocation, no copy) with a doc
      note, so portable code that calls \c features.pinnedHost().toDevice(...)
      still works correctly.

    Apple Silicon device support is gated by \c MLCapabilities.has_metal in
    @ref Qore::ML::ml_get_capabilities() "ml_get_capabilities()". When
    \c has_metal is False (non-Apple builds), \c Tensor::toDevice("metal") raises
    \c ML-TENSOR-DEVICE-ERROR with a clear message.

    @code{.py}
%requires ml

if (!ML::ml_get_capabilities().has_metal) {
    return;   # not Apple Silicon, no Metal/UMA backend
}

# Upload a host tensor to a Metal MTLBuffer (MTLStorageModeShared): zero copy on UMA
ML::Tensor features = new ML::Tensor(data: new buffer<float32>(my_floats), shape: (n, d));
ML::Tensor on_gpu  = features.toDevice("metal", 0);
printf("kind=%s, transfers will stay 0 on UMA\n", on_gpu.deviceInfo().kind);

# Bind a Metal-tagged input to a CoreML model.  inputDeviceMatchesProvider() returns
# true for Metal on any provider; the binding goes through CPU memory info because
# the UMA bytes are already host-addressable.  zero_copy_inputs increments;
# host_to_device_transfers stays at 0.
ML::OnnxModel m("/path/to/model.onnx", <ML::OnnxSessionConfig>{
    "providers": (<ML::OnnxProviderConfig>{"name": "CoreML"},),
    "device_binding": <ML::OnnxDeviceBindingConfig>{"enabled": True},
});
ML::OnnxIoBinding b = m.createBinding();
b.bindInput("X", on_gpu);
b.bindOutput("Y");
ML::Tensor y = b.run(tensors: True).Y;

hash<auto> tr = m.getInferenceStats().device_binding;
printf("zero-copy=%d, transfers (H2D/D2H)=%d/%d, provider_host=%d\n",
    tr.zero_copy_inputs, tr.host_to_device_transfers,
    tr.device_to_host_transfers, tr.provider_host_resolutions);
    @endcode

    Provider-managed device outputs are requested through the reusable I/O binding with
    @ref Qore::ML::OnnxIoBinding::bindOutputsDevice() "bindOutputsDevice()" or
    @ref Qore::ML::OnnxIoBinding::bindOutputDevice() "bindOutputDevice()". A device-resident
    output is returned as a device-backed @ref Qore::ML::Tensor "Tensor"; reading its host
    values (for example with \c toList()) materializes it once through a provider copy-to-host
    callback. Host/device transfers are observable through the \c device_binding block of
    @ref Qore::ML::OnnxModel::getInferenceStats() "getInferenceStats()" (\c zero_copy_inputs,
    \c host_fallback_inputs, \c device_outputs, \c host_outputs, \c output_materializations,
    \c host_to_device_transfers, \c device_to_host_transfers, and
    \c provider_host_resolutions for EP-acceleration providers that resolve to host).

    @code{.py}
hash<Qore::ML::OnnxSessionConfig> cfg = <Qore::ML::OnnxSessionConfig>{
    "providers": (<Qore::ML::OnnxProviderConfig>{"name": "CUDA", "options": {"device_id": "0"}},),
    "device_binding": <Qore::ML::OnnxDeviceBindingConfig>{
        "enabled": True,
        "default_output_device": "provider",   # keep outputs on the GPU
        "materialize_outputs": False,           # do not copy back to host automatically
        "allow_host_fallback": False,           # raise instead of silently using CPU memory
    },
};
Qore::ML::OnnxModel model("/path/to/model.onnx", cfg);

Qore::ML::OnnxIoBinding binding = model.createBinding();
binding.bindInput(name: "X", value: input_tensor);
binding.bindOutputsDevice();                    # provider-managed device output allocation
hash<auto> result = binding.run(tensors: True);
Qore::ML::Tensor output = result.Y;             # device-resident; no host copy yet
printf("device output: %y\n", output.deviceInfo());

# inspect host/device transfer counters
hash<auto> transfers = model.getInferenceStats().device_binding;
printf("device_outputs=%d device->host=%d\n",
    transfers.device_outputs, transfers.device_to_host_transfers);
    @endcode

    Host data can be made device-resident explicitly with
    @ref Qore::ML::Tensor::toDevice() "Tensor::toDevice()", which uploads a tensor to
    accelerator memory (CUDA) and returns a device-backed tensor that binds zero-copy as a
    model input or chains across device inference without round-tripping through host. For
    buffers transferred repeatedly, @ref Qore::ML::Tensor::pinnedHost() "Tensor::pinnedHost()"
    returns a page-locked host copy that uploads over a faster DMA path.

    The same policy is available to higher layers: the DataProviderML
    \c QoreOnnxModelProcessor accepts \c device_binding, \c output_device,
    \c materialize_outputs, \c allow_host_fallback, and \c require_zero_copy options and
    reports transfer counters through its \c getDiagnostics(); QoreModelRegistry execution
    manifests can carry a \c device_binding block that is propagated into the loaded session
    and honored by the model execution adapter.
*/
